       }
      ],
      "text": "Hyperthyroidism:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " adrenergic symptoms may respond; propranolol is used most, atenolol or metoprolol are alternatives. Also for tachycardia, especially in older patients, since antithyroid drugs take weeks to work. Thyroid storm: propranolol cuts HR within 2–3 h PO, minutes IV."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Thyroid storm:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " propranolol 60–80 mg PO q4–6h, adjusted to HR and BP."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Interactions:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " digoxin, clonidine, diltiazem and verapamil add bradycardia (slowed AV conduction); stopping clonidine: withdraw the beta-blocker several days first (rebound HTN). Strong CYP2D6 inhibitors (fluoxetine, paroxetine, quinidine) can double metoprolol levels. Higher epinephrine doses may be needed."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
